**Where phase 2 breaks.** The regional network has about 10,000 (station, line) states. The linear scan re-examines every known state each time it settles one, so a query does up to V²/2 ≈ 50 million map and set operations. The 15-query batch takes several seconds against a 1-second limit, even after the JIT warm-up. The relaxation work itself (about 18,000 connections) is tiny by comparison.

**Optimized approach: `PriorityQueue` with lazy deletion.**

```java
PriorityQueue<State> queue = new PriorityQueue<>(SEARCH_ORDER);
queue.add(start);
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    State current = queue.poll();
    if (!settled.add(current.key)) continue; // stale entry
    ...
    best.put(key, state);
    queue.add(state);
}
```

`SEARCH_ORDER` compares minutes, then transfers, then station and line.

**Complexity.** O((V + E) log V) per query. Each improvement adds at most one entry, so the queue holds O(E) items. The batch drops to a small fraction of the limit.

**Identical results.** The queue orders by (minutes, transfers, station, line), the same total order the linear scan uses. States settle in the same sequence, so even the legs come out identical.

**Tradeoffs and next steps.** Lazy deletion trades memory (stale entries) for simplicity. Hop times are small integers, so a bucket queue (Dial's algorithm) would avoid the log factor. Interning stations and lines as integer ids would avoid hashing records. For a real app, look at RAPTOR or contraction hierarchies, or cache one search per origin when batches share origins.
